<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Newcastle upon Tyne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Newcastle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Newcastle upon Tyne is a city in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a>, named after the castle founded in 1080 on the site of the Roman fort of Pons Aelius. It grew rich from exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, especially to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and was a key northern stronghold against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Scottish Covenanter armies occupied it in 1640 and took it again by storm in 1644; it later became a major industrial and shipbuilding centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne.jpg" alt="Newcastle upon Tyne" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JimmyGuano · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newcastle-upon-Tyne-bridges-and-skyline_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
